rocess in which the genetic information encoded in the DNA sequence is copied into RNA by the enzyme RNA polymerase. This process can be divided into three main stages: initiation, elongation, and termination.\n\nInitiation:\n\nThe first step of transcription is initiation, in which RNA polymerase binds to a specific sequence of DNA called the promoter. The promoter is usually located upstream of the gene that is being transcribed. In bacteria, RNA polymerase uses a sigma factor to recognize and bind to the promoter, while in eukaryotes, several transcription factors are involved in this process. Together, these factors help the RNA polymerase to position itself correctly and begin the process of RNA synthesis.\n\nElongation:\n\nOnce RNA polymerase is bound to the promoter, it starts to unwind the DNA double helix and initiate RNA synthesis. The RNA polymerase reads the template strand of the DNA molecule, synthesizing RNA in the 5' to 3' direction. As the RNA polymerase moves along the DNA, it adds nucleotides sequentially, following the complementarity of the DNA template. This process, known as elongation, continues until the RNA polymerase reaches a termination signal.\n\nTermination:\n\nIn prokaryotes, RNA polymerase stops transcription when it reaches a terminator sequence in the DNA molecule. In eukaryotes, the termination mechanism is more complex, and several mechanisms have been identified.\n\nLeading and Lagging strands:\n\nThe DNA molecule has two strands, the template strand, and the coding strand. The template strand is the one RNA polymerase uses to synthesize RNA, while the coding is the one that carries the information for protein synthesis. Since RNA polymerase can only move in one direction, the DNA template is split into two strands, the leading and the lagging strands. The leading strand is immediately accessible by RNA polymerase, while the lagging strand requires extra processing.\n\nTypes of RNA polymerase:\n\nIn bacteria, there is only one type of RNA polymerase that synthesizes various types of RNA, including mRNA, rRNA, and tRNA. In eukaryotes, there are three types of RNA polymerase:\n\n- RNA polymerase I: This enzyme is responsible for transcribing the genes that encode large ribosomal RNAs.\n- RNA polymerase II: This enzyme transcribes the genes that encode messenger RNAs, as well as some small non-coding RNAs.\n- RNA ","tokens":["<","|","im","_","start","|>","system","\n","You"," are"," a"," helpful"," assistant",".<","|","im","_","end","|>","\n","<","|","im","_","start","|>","user","\n","Ex","plain"," step","-","by","-","step"," the"," biochemical"," and"," molecular"," process"," of"," DNA"," transcription",","," including"," the"," enzymes"," involved",","," the"," initiation"," and"," elongation"," of"," RNA"," synthesis",","," and"," the"," regulation"," of"," gene"," expression"," during"," transcription","."," Be"," sure"," to"," explain"," the"," differences"," between"," the"," leading"," and"," lag","ging"," strands",","," the"," types"," of"," RNA"," polymerase",","," and"," the"," role"," of"," transcription"," factors"," and"," enh","ancers","/","prom","ot","ers","."," Prov","ide"," examples"," of"," how"," gene"," expression"," is"," regulated"," and"," how"," errors"," in"," transcription"," may"," lead"," to"," genetic"," mutations",".<","|","im","_","end","|>","\n","<","|","im","_","start","|>","ass","istant","\n","Trans","cription"," is"," the"," process"," in"," which"," the"," genetic"," information"," encoded"," in"," the"," DNA"," sequence"," is"," copied"," into"," RNA"," by"," the"," enzyme"," RNA"," polymerase","."," This"," process"," can"," be"," divided"," into"," three"," main"," stages",":"," initiation",","," elongation",","," and"," termination",".","\n","\n","Init","iation",":","\n","\n","The"," first"," step"," of"," transcription"," is"," initiation",","," in"," which"," RNA"," polymerase"," binds"," to"," a"," specific"," sequence"," of"," DNA"," called"," the"